            D, so the first build of each needs an existing compiler, like
            stage 0 needed the host's gcc and rust/go needed their stage0
            tarballs.  gcc's 3-stage bootstrap builds stages 2 and 3 with our
            own compiler and compares them, so the shipped gnat1/cc1d are
            ours; the seeds only build stage 1.  Decision: go on (LFS level:
            host compiler + seeds for the self-hosting languages, written in
            the README as the only exceptions).
            For the end (handbook chapter, optional): fewer seeds.  D from
            gcc 11 (last C++ D front end), Go from the go1.4 C chain, Rust
            from mrustc (as Guix does).  Ada: no known source-only path;
            verify before writing that down.

