2026-10-15  FAIL  gcc (pass 2): "configure: error: GNAT is required to build
                   ada" (log line 70; the mv errors after it are the
                   SlackBuild packaging an empty build, as in stage 1).
            check  gnat1 is in /usr/libexec/gcc/x86_64-slackware-linux/16.2.0
                   (seed), but "gcc -c t.adb" says "t.adb: Ada compiler not
                   installed on this system".  The D check passed (gdc is a
                   driver of its own).
            cause  gcc's configure (ACX_PROG_GNAT) needs ${CC} -c conftest.adb
                   to work.  The driver knows .adb/.ads only from the
                   lang-specs.h of the languages it was built with; the pass 1
                   gcc had no Ada, so its table has the "#Ada" placeholder.
                   On Pat's machine the installed gcc was built with Ada.
            fix    tools/gnat-bootstrap-cc + overrides/all/gcc.  From the
                   gcc source's own gcc/ada/gcc-interface/lang-specs.h (and
                   DUMPS_OPTIONS from gcc/gcc.cc) it compiles a tiny program
                   that prints the Ada entries as a specs file, writes a
                   "/usr/bin/gcc -specs=<that file>" wrapper and exports CC
                   to it before configure.  gcc.cc read_specs adds non-'*'
                   entries of a specs file to the compilers table and
                   lookup_compiler searches it from the end, so they win over
                   "#Ada" (checked in gcc-16.2.0's gcc.cc).  Only stage 1 of
                   the bootstrap uses CC; stages 2/3 use the new xgcc, which
                   has Ada built in.  Nothing is left on the system (the
                   files live in $TMP).  No-op when gcc already knows Ada and
                   when ada is not in LANGS (stage 1).
            then   configure passed ("compiler driver understands Ada ...
                   yes"), stage 1 stopped: gcc/ada/Make-generated.in runs
                   "gnatmake ... gen_il-main" and gnatmake calls plain "gcc"
                   from PATH (not $CC): "gen_il-main.adb: Ada compiler not
                   installed".  The wrapper is now bin/gcc in the work dir
                   and the tool exports PATH=<that bin>:$PATH as well as CC.
            then   the same error with PATH set (628 lines in the log name the
                   wrapper, so it ran).  gnatmake does look "gcc" up in PATH
                   (make.adb Locate_Exec_On_Path, adaint.c getenv PATH), so
                   why the wrapper was not used is not known; not chased.
            fix 2  tools/gnat-bootstrap-specs replaces gnat-bootstrap-cc (gone
                   from the kit).  Instead of a wrapper it appends the Ada
                   entries to the installed gcc's own specs file
                   (gcc -print-file-name=specs; Pat's gcc package ships one
                   at /usr/lib64/gcc/x86_64-slackware-linux/16.2.0/specs), so
                   every way of running gcc knows Ada.  "on" saves the file
                   in /var/lib/sfs/gnat-bootstrap/ and checks gcc -c and a
                   gnatmake build+run of a small Ada program; "off" puts the
                   file back; the override runs "on" before configure and
                   "off" from an EXIT trap of the build subshell; "on" first
                   undoes an interrupted run.  The new gcc package replaces
                   the specs file anyway.
                   Tested here with Ubuntu's gcc 13 + gnat 13 and the 16.2.0
                   lang-specs.h (SFS_FORCE=1 skips the "already knows Ada"
                   check): -specs and the default specs file both override
                   the built-in entry; on/off/interrupted restore the file
                   byte for byte.  Found on the way: a second blank line
                   before the appended entries gives "specs file malformed".
            result gcc pass 2 built (about 70 min, -j20): gcc, g++, gfortran,
                   gnat, gdc, go, gm2, objc, rust, gcobol, all 16.2.0-2; the
                   seeds are replaced by our own gcc-gnat and gcc-gdc.

