2026-10-16  Kernel series: the user's choice, nothing hardcoded (user: "the
            user must be able to choose, the tool must not decide"; the
            development branch has k612/ and k72/ today, tomorrow it may have
            one, or other names).
            - sfs-kernels.sh (new): a kernel series is any directory of the
              tree with kernel-*.SlackBuild in it; lists them with the
              version of their linux tarball, plus the kernel packages of
              slackware64/.  Sourced by sfs-plan.sh and sfs-stage0.sh
              (sfs_check_kseries): they stop with the list when KSERIES is
              empty or not a kernel series.  sfs.conf.example: KSERIES= (no
              default; it was k612).
            - sfs-plan.sh: every SCOPE series must exist; the only kernel
              series in SCOPE must be KSERIES.
            - gen.py: a package name made by two units resolves to the unit
              whose series is in SCOPE (was: first alphabetically, so
              "kernel-headers" deps went to k612 even with k72); stops when
              two series of the scope make the same package, or a scope
              series has no SlackBuilds.
            - sfs-deps-export.py: same resolution, to the unit we built.
            Tested on a copy: KSERIES empty / unknown / two kernels in SCOPE
            / KSERIES not in SCOPE / unknown series all stop with a message;
            k72 and k612 give the same plan apart from the series name.
            Open: building from testing/ (design after seeing the tree's
            testing/ layout).

